Before coding, state your assumptions explicitly. If ambiguous, present interpretations and ask — don't pick silently. Push back if a simpler approach exists.
Write minimum code. No abstractions, no extra features, no configurability, no error handling for impossible cases unless asked. If existing code could be simpler, rewrite it. Ask yourself: would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated? If yes, simplify.
Only touch what the task requires. Match existing style. If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it — don't delete it. Remove only imports/variables YOUR changes made unused.
For complex tasks, write a plan first: each step with a verifiable check. Transform tasks into goals — write a test that defines success, then meet it. Loop until success criteria are met.
